{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfi6whbds1gJQUCUerohR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ticket 05 is done and committed in two commits. Every suite the ticket names passes: `npm test` (217 browser tests), `test:ssr` (60), `test:hydration` and `test:hydration-dev`, plus `test:plugins` (13) and `npm run size`.\n\n[x] Bundling for the browser resolves `microtags/htm` to the browser module and bundling for Node resolves the server module\n[x] `mount` into an empty container creates the elements the markup describes, including a kept component whose host upgrades\n[x] A signal passed as a component prop updates the host when the signal changes, with no attribute write required\n[x] A signal bound to a plain element attribute updates it; `false`, `null`, and `undefined` remove the attribute\n[x] An `on<event>` function fires on a plain element and on a host\n[x] After dispose, signal changes no longer update the DOM, listeners no longer fire, and the nodes are still in the container\n[x] Mounting a second time on the same container throws\n[x] Creating a component with no render link throws an error that contains its tag name\n[x] A consumer who imports only `microtags` gets none of this (`microtags` size entry unchanged); `npm run size` passes with the new entry\n[x] `npm test`, `npm run test:ssr`, `npm run test:hydration`, and `npm run test:hydration-dev` pass\n\n**What I built:**\n1. **`src/htm.browser.ts`** exports `html`, `h`, `raw`, `Markup` and `mount`. In the browser, `html` returns a description of the nodes rather than a string, but `String(m)` still produces static HTML. That keeps the client's existing `String(html\\`<${Notice}>\\`)` call and kept components' render functions working.\n2. **Creating a component:** `mount` calls the component's static `render`, parses the result with `importNode` (which upgrades the host right away), then assigns props through the host's accessors. Everything runs inside an `effectScope`, so dispose stops the effects and removes the listeners `mount` added.\n3. **Packaging:**\n   - `exports[\"./htm\"]` now has a `browser` condition first.\n   - The new file is added to all three browser build scripts.\n   - The new size entry is set to 3.4 kB; it measured 3.33 kB.\n   - The `microtags` entry is unchanged at 4.15 kB.\n4. **Example's Vite config:** a small `resolveId` plugin sends `microtags/htm` to the server or browser source depending on `ssr`. The `microtags` alias is now an exact-match regex so it no longer catches `microtags/htm`. I checked that the client build contains the browser module.\n5. **Shared type:** `RenderFn` in `builders.ts` now accepts both `Markup` types (a type-only import).\n\n**Things to know:**\n- **TDD order:** I wrote the module before its tests, so I didn't get a true red step. To make up for it, I broke the prop assignment and the listener removal on purpose and confirmed the matching tests failed (3 failures).\n- **Children of a component** are turned into a static HTML string on the create path, so listeners inside them are lost. Reactive regions and adoption are tickets 06 and 07.\n- **Types for `mount`:** TypeScript doesn't apply the `browser` condition by default, so consumers' type checking still sees the server types, which don't include `mount`. A later ticket may need `customConditions` or a type stub.\n- **Lint:** `npm run lint` reports two unused-variable errors in `test/plugins.ts` (lines 105 and 214). They were there before this change and I didn't touch them.\n- **Setup:** the worktree had no `node_modules`, so I ran `npm install`.\n\nI added notes on the browser templater to `AGENTS.md`.\n\nSeparately, the Gmail, Google Calendar, Google Drive and Netlify connectors need authorizing in your claude.ai connector settings, and the aws-mcp server failed to connect. This work didn't use any of them."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,